ABSTRACT
This paper proposes a formal analogical framework drawn from electromagnetic physics —
specifically the principle of mutual inductance — to model the dynamics of human relational
systems. The central argument is that human interactions exhibit structural properties that are not
merely metaphorically similar to electromagnetic coupling, but formally isomorphic in their
behavioral signatures: energy transfer, resonance, phase alignment, field interference, and the
decay of coupling across distance and misalignment.
We introduce a precise vocabulary — mutual inductance, conglomerate, up-relationship, draining
system, Dirac signal, notch intervention, and clock cycles — as technical terms within this
framework, each carrying formal definitional content rather than serving as rhetorical devices. We
argue that the adoption of physics-derived vocabulary in human science is not a category error but
a methodological advance: where conventional psychological and sociological models rely on
narrative description, the physics-derived framework yields structural predictions about relational
behavior that are testable, falsifiable, and intervention-relevant.
The framework is applied across three scales: dyadic interaction, organizational dynamics, and
civilizational structure. At each scale, the mutual inductance model yields non-obvious predictions
that conventional relational theory fails to generate. We conclude with a methodological argument

for formal analogy as a legitimate and underutilized tool in interaction science, and with
implications for the design of AI systems intended to support human relational health.
